  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nThis Evening and Late Saturday: Low Potential for Light Rain\n\nThis afternoon/evening, weak shortwave looks to ride the upper ridge \nthat has been keeping us warm and dry. This feature, along with some \nmodest moist advection at 850mb, could (10-20% per 23.00z NBM/HREF \nand 22.12z LREF) produce a light shower as the shortwave passes over \nareas north of Interstate 90. No hazards are expected.\n\nMoving into the weekend, strong northern stream upper low barrels \neastward over Canada with the associated cold front pushing south \nthrough the Upper Midwest. However, with this feature failing to \nphase with a southern stream upper low that looks to linger over the \nFour Corners, the front will eventually stall somewhere near our \nCWA. As this occurs, mid-level moisture will continue to be advected \nnorthward on the western side of a ridge centered in the Ohio \nValley. Thus, as weak shortwaves eject northeast from the Four \nCorners upper low, some light rain could result given the \ncombination of modest cooling aloft, mid level moisture, and lift \nassociated with the stalled front. Think its a pretty good bet some \nspots will pick up some rain but most guidance keeps best moist \nfetch just to our northwest. Have therefore carried some low (10-\n20%) PoPs in our area for now.\n\nSunday - Wednesday: Greater Precipitation Potential, Trending Cooler\n\nGreater precipitation potential exists Monday through Tuesday as the \ncutoff low across the Desert Southwest begins to lift northward. How \nthis feature evolves as it moves northward and where the heaviest \nrainfall associated with it will occur remains rather uncertain \nthough. The GEFS/AIGEFS members largely suggest the low takes a more \nsouthern trajectory, keeping higher probabilities for precipitation \nacross a majority of the Upper Mississippi River Valley (60-80%) \nwhereas the EPS/AIFS Ens on the other hand suggest a more northern \nand western solution, keeping higher probabilities across Minnesota \ninto northern Wisconsin. Given the uncertainty, probabilities for \nprecipitation remain on the lower end, currently sitting around 30-\n40% in the 23.01z NBM though these probabilities may increase as the \nmodels begin to converge on a solution. \n\nOther than precipitation potential, the passage of the quasi- \nstationary front on Sunday will allow cooler temperatures to begin \nfiltering into the Upper Midwest. There is a great deal of spread \nwithin the 25th-75th and 10th-90th percentiles of the ensemble \nguidance related to the temperature forecast Monday through \nWednesday however, around 10 and 20 degrees respectively, due in no \nsmall part to the different scenarios mentioned above. That said, \nthe overall trend is towards more seasonable temperatures through \nthe beginning of the new week.",
